Prompt caching for the Anthropic provider, Open Agent Spec CLI 1.6.0. The
provider sends `system` as a plain string and the messages untouched, so every
round of a tool loop pays again for the system prompt, the tool definitions and
the whole incident view. This adds two cache breakpoints: one at the end of the
system block, which covers the tools with it because Anthropic orders the
prefix tools, system, messages; and one at the end of the prompt's last
user turn, which is where the view sits. Not the first turn: the runner
prepends any supplied history, and on that path the first turn is an old
message while the view goes uncached on every round. The last user turn that
carries no tool_result is the same message on every round, which is what a
breakpoint needs.

Apply the tool-messages patch beside this one first. Both are cut against the
1.6.0 wheel and both apply with no fuzz in that order:

    patch "$(python3 -c 'import oas_cli.providers.anthropic_http as m; print(m.__file__)')" \
        < patches/oas-1.6.0-anthropic-tool-messages.diff
    patch "$(python3 -c 'import oas_cli.providers.anthropic_http as m; print(m.__file__)')" \
        < patches/oas-1.6.0-anthropic-prompt-caching.diff

Set `intelligence.config.cache_prompt: false` in a spec to switch it off.

WHY THE SECOND BREAKPOINT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS. Counted with Anthropic's
token counter against this repository's own specs: the Precedent Analyst's
system prompt is about 1,700 tokens and the bridge view it is handed is about
22,000, so a system-only breakpoint covers roughly seven per cent of a recall
request and four per cent of an assess. The first user turn is the rest.

HOW TO CHECK A HIT, AND WHY NOT WITH `oa run`. The CLI keeps `input_tokens` and
`output_tokens` and drops the rest, so `oa run` never shows
`cache_read_input_tokens` at all, in either direction: a run that caches
nothing and a run that caches everything look identical through it. Read the
cache fields off the API instead. `checks/cache_probe.py` sends two identical
requests through the installed provider's own helpers and prints both usage
blocks; it costs two small calls and needs a key, so it is a command you run
rather than something CI can. It also checks the eligibility rule itself, which
the live half cannot see: that request is oversized and carries no tools, so it
would pass whether or not the floor were measured correctly. Those cases need no
key and run without one.

Verified 23 Aug 2026 that way, against an install rebuilt from the 1.6.0 wheel
through both patches: `checks/cache_probe.py` reported cache_creation 13,006 on
the first call and cache_read 13,006 on the second, with two breakpoints in the
request. Separately, one live recall through the patched provider made twelve
tool calls over six queries and returned the same three precedents as the
unpatched runs.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. It does not move a breakpoint with the
conversation, so the tool results a long recall accumulates are still re-sent
uncached. That is worth more again, and worth measuring on its own rather than
being inherited from this. It also leaves single-shot calls' message content
alone: with no second round there is nothing to reuse, and a sweep sends a
different view per case.

THE THRESHOLD IS PER MODEL, and it started out wrong. The minimum cacheable
prefix is in tokens, roughly 1,024 for Sonnet and Opus and 2,048 for Haiku, and
the first version of this patch used a flat 4,096 characters for all of them.
That admitted the commander's system prompt, which is 4,333 characters and
1,018 tokens: a breakpoint the model would never cache, on the one call in this
repository that is single-shot. Not an error, and not a saving either, which is
the kind of setting that reads like one in a diff.

The floor is now the model's own minimum converted at four characters to the
token with a fifteen per cent margin, so 4,710 characters for Sonnet and Opus
and 9,420 for Haiku, and it is measured against the whole prefix ahead of the
breakpoint rather than the block being marked. Anthropic's minimum applies to
tools then system then messages, so a spec with a short system prompt and
substantial tool definitions clears it on the pair and on neither half; the
first version measured each block alone and would have declined a breakpoint
that had every right to cache. On this repository's own specs nothing moves,
because the only tool-bound agent already cleared the floor on its system
prompt alone. The rule was wrong in general, which is enough. Of the six specs here only the Precedent Analyst's system
prompt clears it, which is the agent whose tool loop re-sends it five to ten
times a run and the reason this patch exists. The commander's does not, and its
single-shot `assess` therefore sets no breakpoint at all rather than one that
does nothing.

ONE INTERACTION WITH `evals/`. A judgement record carries a provider
fingerprint, and this changes the provider, so arms recorded before it and
after it are not the same configuration twice. Comparisons across that boundary
are not like for like, and `--check-records` is where that shows up.

